## Rows skipped (with reason)
bn-a 1  (P1) "কিছু রায়ে প্রায় 3 মাস …; এটি কোনো নিশ্চিত বা সাধারণ ফল নয়।" — the reviewer asks to restore
        "courts currently GENERALLY impose about 3 months" and to drop "সাধারণ ফল নয়". That is precisely the wording
        RULEBOOK-FIX R3 mandates removing: 'courts currently impose about 3 months / 4 months / 6 months →
        "in some judgments about 3 months has been imposed; there is no typical or guaranteed outcome"'.
        The two are logically contradictory ("generally about 3 months" vs "no typical outcome"), so the supervisor's
        R3 form was kept. Same for the 4-month / 6-month sentence. Flagging for the supervisor: if R3 is relaxed for
        this locale, the reviewer's sentence is the better Bengali.
bn-a 13 (P2) "TWD 1.57M" → "TWD 15,70,000" and lakh grouping generally — skipped twice over:
        R12 / ACCEPTED-ROUND pin "TWD 1.57M" byte-identical (src/data/__tests__/guidance-team-bios.test.ts), and
        R14 / ACCEPTED-ROUND forbid lakh/crore grouping because the checker cannot parse it. Western grouping kept
        in 001 / 002 / 003 / 004 / 010 / 011 as before.
bn-a 30 (P2) 004 source-list label "অনুচ্ছেদ 3 (ব্যবসায়িক কর আইনের অনুচ্ছেদ 10 নয়)" — tried removing the parenthetical;
        the checker then fails: `numbers FAIL missing from translation (1): 10`, because the Korean source label is
        "영업세법 제10조" and the digit 10 is counted. Per the WO ("prefer the form the checker accepts") the original
        form was restored. All 15 other locales checked (de, fr, es, ru, pl, th, vi, id, ar, it, nl, sv, cs, tr, pt)
        keep the 10 in that label too. Needs a source-side fix (the Korean label itself is wrong), not a bn-side one.
bn-b 20 (P2) U+200B removal in 013 (5) and 015 (3) — the checker pins ZWSP count against src/content/columns
        (the Korean source), which contains exactly 5 in 013 and 3 in 015; removing any fails `blocks`. Four of the
        five in 013 and all three in 015 are lines whose only character is the ZWSP, i.e. they are blocks. The
        reviewer's premise ("the English sources contain none") is true but the gate compares against the Korean
        source, not the English one. Needs a source-side decision.

